## 7. Does concentration of subdivision vertices correlate with larger \(b(T)\)?

### [DIRECTLY FROM DATA]

There is no simple monotone relationship in these 11 cases.

Using the maximum subdivision length \(\max_i s_i\):

| \(\max_i s_i\) | Patterns | \(b(T)\) values |
| -------------: | -------: | --------------- |
|              1 |        1 | 3               |
|              2 |        3 | 3, 3, **4**     |
|              3 |        3 | 3, 3, 3         |
|              4 |        2 | 3, 3            |
|              5 |        1 | 3               |
|              6 |        1 | 3               |

In particular:

* the most concentrated pattern \((6,0,\ldots,0)\) has \(b(T)=3\);
* \((5,1,0,\ldots,0)\) has \(b(T)=3\);
* \((4,2,0,\ldots,0)\) has \(b(T)=3\);
* the unique \(b(T)=4\) case has only \(\max_i s_i=2\).

So, for this finite \(K_{1,9}\), \(\sum s_i=6\) experiment:

$$
\boxed{\text{maximum subdivision length alone does not determine }b(T).}
$$

### [STRUCTURAL HYPOTHESIS]

The exceptional behavior appears to depend on the distribution of subdivision mass across several branches rather than simply on making one branch very long.

This is only a hypothesis suggested by the finite data.

