## 8. Does the number of subdivided edges matter?

### [DIRECTLY FROM DATA]

The number of subdivided edges is not sufficient by itself to determine \(b(T)\).

| Number of subdivided edges | Patterns | \(b(T)\) values |
| -------------------------: | -------: | --------------- |
|                          1 |        1 | 3               |
|                          2 |        3 | 3               |
|                          3 |        3 | 3, 3, **4**     |
|                          4 |        2 | 3               |
|                          5 |        1 | 3               |
|                          6 |        1 | 3               |

The unique \(b(T)=4\) pattern has exactly 3 subdivided edges, but two other patterns with exactly 3 subdivided edges have \(b(T)=3\):

$$
(4,1,1),
\qquad
(3,2,1).
$$

Therefore:

$$
\boxed{\text{number of subdivided edges alone is not enough}.}
$$

### [STRUCTURAL HYPOTHESIS]

The precise sizes of the subdivisions, together with their distribution over the branches, appear more relevant than the number of affected edges alone.

This has not been established beyond these 11 cases.

